    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Thus, if inbreeding depression is primarily a consequence of overdominance, then the ratio $ (A + B)/A $ is constrained to be less than or equal to two in populations that are in a state of balanced polymorphism. Strictly speaking, this result applies to a diallelic locus. With k alleles per locus, the constraint is $ (A + B)/A \\leq k $ (Crow 1958, Lewontin 1974). Returning to Table 10.4, we see that this ratio is usually on the order of 10 or more. Thus, unless a very large number of alleles are maintained in a delicately balanced polymorphism at each locus contributing to inbreeding depression, which seems quite unlikely, the results from lethal-equivalent analyses seem generally inconsistent with the overdominance model.",
      "source_page": 298,
